The program's path from the auxiliary vector

PHP's CLI finds the folder of its php.ini from where its program is
(PHP_BINARY). A command of a Dukovo bundle is run by the program's path in
the bundle's folder, which may be in /applications or in a person's own
applications folder, with only the command's name as argv[0], so a search
of PATH finds the link bundled(8) made, which does not lead there. The
kernel gives the path a program was run as in the auxiliary vector
(AT_EXECFN), as Linux does: this takes it as the program when it is
absolute, and its real path as PHP_BINARY, as Linux's is. For a script
whose #! line names PHP, AT_EXECFN is the script's path, as on Linux, so a
file that starts with "#!" is not taken: argv[0], the interpreter's path
from the #! line, is.

